// emit: llvm-ir
// opt: both
// The panic function begins with the guard that stops a second panic (ADR 0121):
// a volatile read of `__erebos_panicking`, an `i8` that is 0 until the first
// panic, a block that branches to itself when it is not 0, and otherwise a
// volatile write of 1 and the body. The flag is zero-initialized and private to
// the object. The panic function is `noinline`, so that one copy of it, with one
// guard, exists. Here it takes no parameter; `divide` shows that nothing else
// changes: a check calls the panic function directly, as before.
fn divide(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {
    a / b
}

entry fn main() -> ! { loop {} }
panic fn on_panic() -> ! { loop {} }
